    "overview": "Electronic Stratego (1982) is a computerized version of the classic hidden-information strategy game, built by Milton Bradley. Its defining interaction is a sensory board: each of the 40 playing pieces has a unique series of bumps molded into its base (a mechanical binary encoding), and the battery-operated, touch-sensitive board reads the bump pattern to identify which piece was placed on which square. Placing a physical piece onto a square IS the input — there is no coordinate keypad. The board enforces the game's hidden-state mechanics automatically, revealing a piece's rank only during combat resolution via LEDs and sound.\n\nThis makes the artifact a computational referee for hidden information: in regular Stratego, players must manually track their pieces' secret ranks and compare them on attack; the electronic version outsources that bookkeeping to the sensing board. It also replaced the cardboard bomb tokens with detectable hidden bomb pegs. The bump-pattern encoding is an electromechanical solution reminiscent of early pinball and telephone-exchange mechanical logic.",
    "deepDive": [
      {
        "heading": "Piece-as-input via bump encoding",
        "text": "The interaction model is the point: the physical act of placing a piece on the board both identifies it and positions it, in one action. The mechanical bumps under each piece are read by contact switches in the recessed squares — a tangible, invisible encoding that makes the game board the entire interface."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Hidden state enforced by the machine",
        "text": "The board automatically keeps each player's piece ranks secret and only reveals the winner of a battle. The machine, not the players' memory, maintains the hidden information — a role normally played by flipping pieces by hand. This is a genuinely strange and prescient use of a sensory board."
      }
